    const passage1 = "A short history of the umbrella\n\nThe umbrella is so familiar an object that it is easy to forget how long and varied its history has been. The earliest known umbrellas were not designed to keep off rain at all, but to provide shade from the sun. Carvings from ancient Egypt and Assyria, dating back more than three thousand years, show servants holding parasols over the heads of kings and nobles. In these societies, the parasol was a symbol of power and status, and ordinary people were rarely permitted to use one.\n\nIn China, the umbrella seems to have taken a more practical turn. By the fourth century, Chinese craftsmen were making umbrellas from oiled paper stretched over bamboo frames, which allowed them to be used in wet weather. Some historians credit the Chinese with inventing the collapsible umbrella, although the evidence for precisely when this happened is limited. What is clear is that umbrellas spread from China to Korea and Japan, where they became an important element of court ceremony.\n\nIn Europe, the umbrella had a more uncertain history. The ancient Greeks and Romans used parasols, mainly as fashion accessories for women, but after the fall of the Roman Empire they largely disappeared from everyday use in Western Europe. They re-emerged in Italy and France in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, again chiefly as sunshades for wealthy women. For a man to carry one was considered effeminate.\n\nThe figure most often associated with changing this attitude in England is Jonas Hanway, a merchant and philanthropist who had travelled widely in Persia. From the 1750s, he made a habit of carrying an umbrella through the streets of London, despite being jeered at by passers-by. Coachmen were particularly hostile, since they feared that if people could stay dry while walking, fewer would pay for a carriage ride. Hanway persisted for some thirty years, and by the time of his death in 1786, the umbrella was becoming an accepted item for men as well as women. For a time, umbrellas in England were even known as ‘Hanways’.\n\nEarly European umbrellas were heavy and cumbersome. Their frames were made of wood or whalebone, and their covers of oiled canvas, which became even heavier when wet. A significant improvement came in 1852, when Samuel Fox, an English manufacturer, designed a frame with steel ribs. Fox had a large stock of steel left over from making corsets, and he realised it could be put to new use. His design was lighter, stronger and cheaper to produce, and it established the basic structure that most umbrellas still follow today.\n\nThe twentieth century brought further innovations. In 1928, Hans Haupt, an Austrian student, developed a compact folding umbrella that could fit into a bag. Later decades saw the introduction of nylon covers, automatic opening mechanisms and designs intended to withstand strong winds. Despite these advances, the umbrella remains surprisingly fragile; millions are thrown away every year, many after only a few uses, and because they combine metal, plastic and fabric, very few are recycled.\n\nToday, umbrellas are produced on an enormous scale, with the majority manufactured in China. Yet the object has never entirely lost its symbolic associations. In some parts of Asia and Africa, ornate ceremonial umbrellas are still used to honour religious figures and leaders, a reminder of the parasol’s ancient role as a mark of status.";
    const passage2 = "How animals sleep\n\nA  Every animal studied so far appears to sleep in some form, from elephants to fruit flies. Yet sleep is, on the face of it, a dangerous activity. A sleeping animal is unable to look for food, find a mate or watch for predators. That sleep has survived millions of years of evolution suggests that it must provide benefits important enough to outweigh these risks.\n\nB  The amount of sleep animals need varies enormously. Brown bats may sleep for up to twenty hours a day, while giraffes in the wild often manage less than five, taken in short naps of a few minutes at a time. In general, large plant-eating animals sleep less than predators, perhaps because they need to spend so much of the day eating and because they are more vulnerable when lying down.\n\nC  Some species have evolved remarkable ways of sleeping while remaining alert. Dolphins and some seals can rest one half of the brain at a time, while the other half stays awake, allowing them to keep swimming and to surface for air. Certain birds use the same technique, particularly when they sleep at the edge of a group, where the risk from predators is highest; they keep the eye facing outwards open.\n\nD  Perhaps the most extreme example comes from frigatebirds, seabirds that may spend weeks flying over the ocean without landing. By attaching small recording devices to the birds, researchers discovered that they do sleep in flight, but only for around forty minutes a day, in bursts lasting a few seconds. On land, the same birds sleep for more than twelve hours. How they cope with so little sleep at sea remains a mystery.\n\nE  Why animals sleep is still debated. One theory proposes that sleep conserves energy, by allowing the body to rest during periods when activity would be less productive. Another suggests that sleep enables the brain to clear away waste products that build up while we are awake. A third links sleep to memory: experiments with rats have shown that patterns of brain activity recorded while the animals explored a maze are replayed during sleep.\n\nF  Studies of animals deprived of sleep provide some support for the idea that sleep is essential. Fruit flies kept awake for long periods show impaired learning, and in some experiments prolonged sleep loss has proved fatal. However, interpreting such studies is difficult, since the methods used to keep animals awake can themselves cause stress, which may be responsible for some of the effects observed.\n\nG  Research on animal sleep is not merely of academic interest. Because the basic mechanisms of sleep appear to be shared across many species, findings from simple organisms such as fruit flies and zebrafish can shed light on human sleep disorders. Several genes linked to sleep in humans were first identified in flies, and scientists hope that this work will eventually lead to new treatments for insomnia.";
    const passage3 = "Learning from failure in the science classroom\n\nFor generations, science in schools has been taught largely as a body of established facts. Students learn that water boils at 100 degrees, that the Earth orbits the Sun, and that living things are made of cells. Practical lessons, where they exist, typically involve following a set of instructions to reach a result that is already known. If the experiment ‘fails’, the student is usually assumed to have made a mistake. This approach, I would argue, gives a fundamentally misleading impression of what science is.\n\nReal science is untidy. Most experiments do not work as planned, and researchers spend far more time dealing with unexpected results than confirming predictions. Some of the most important discoveries in history — penicillin, X-rays, the cosmic microwave background — arose from results that were initially regarded as errors or nuisances. Scientists who are able to recognise the significance of an unexpected observation, rather than dismissing it, are often the ones who make the greatest contributions.\n\nIf schools wish to prepare students for careers in science, or simply to help them understand how scientific knowledge is produced, they need to give students experience of this uncertainty. One way of doing so is through open-ended investigations, in which students design their own experiments to answer questions to which the teacher does not know the answer in advance. Such investigations are inevitably messier than conventional practicals, and they are harder to assess. But they offer something that recipe-style experiments cannot: the experience of not knowing, and of having to decide what to do next.\n\nCritics argue that this approach is unrealistic. Teachers already struggle to cover the content required by national curricula, and open-ended work is time-consuming. There is also a risk that students, faced with confusing results, will simply become frustrated and lose interest. These concerns are legitimate, but I believe they are overstated. Research in several countries has found that students who carry out open investigations are, if anything, more motivated than those who follow set instructions, provided that they receive adequate support. The key is to treat unexpected results as a normal and interesting part of the process, rather than as a sign that something has gone wrong.\n\nAssessment is a more serious obstacle. Examination systems tend to reward correct answers, and students quickly learn that the safest strategy is to produce the result the examiner expects. Some exam boards have experimented with assessing students’ ability to evaluate their methods and explain anomalies, rather than simply recording the ‘right’ result. These reforms are a step in the right direction, but they remain the exception rather than the rule.\n\nNone of this means that factual knowledge is unimportant. Students cannot investigate the world effectively without a grasp of established scientific ideas. The point is that facts and processes should be taught together. A student who knows that water boils at 100 degrees but has never wondered why the reading on their thermometer shows 98 has learned only half the lesson.\n\nUltimately, teaching students to value failure may have benefits beyond science. In a world that often rewards the appearance of certainty, the ability to admit that one does not know, to examine evidence honestly and to change one’s mind accordingly is a skill worth cultivating in everyone.";
