    const passage1 = "The return of the tram\n\nIn the first half of the twentieth century, trams were a common sight in cities across Europe and North America. By the 1960s, however, most had disappeared. Cities removed their tram networks in favour of buses and private cars, which were seen as more modern and flexible. Tram tracks were regarded as an obstacle to traffic, and the vehicles themselves were often old and poorly maintained.\n\nIn recent decades, the tram has made a remarkable comeback. Since 1985, more than a hundred new tram systems have opened in Europe alone, and others have been built in cities as diverse as Dubai, Casablanca and Sydney. In France, where the revival has been particularly strong, cities such as Strasbourg, Bordeaux and Montpellier have built extensive networks that have transformed their city centres.\n\nSeveral factors explain this revival. Modern trams are quieter, more comfortable and more accessible than their predecessors, with low floors that allow passengers in wheelchairs or with pushchairs to board easily. Because they run on electricity, they produce no exhaust emissions in the street, an important advantage at a time of growing concern about air quality. They can also carry large numbers of passengers: a single tram may hold up to 300 people.\n\nPerhaps more importantly, trams have become a tool of urban regeneration. In many cities, the construction of a tram line has been accompanied by the redesign of the streets along its route. Roads that were once dominated by cars have been turned into pedestrian zones, with trees, benches and wider pavements. In Strasbourg, the opening of the first line in 1994 was linked to a policy of removing through traffic from the historic centre, a decision that was controversial at the time but is now widely regarded as a success.\n\nResearchers have also identified what they call the ‘rail factor’: the tendency of people to prefer rail-based transport to buses, even when journey times are similar. Surveys suggest that passengers perceive trams as more reliable and easier to understand, partly because the tracks make the route visible. As a result, new tram lines often attract people who previously travelled by car, whereas new bus services tend mainly to attract existing public transport users.\n\nTrams are not without their critics. Construction is expensive and disruptive; building a single kilometre of track can cost tens of millions of euros, and the work can cause months of disruption to local businesses. Some economists argue that the same money would deliver greater benefits if spent on improving bus networks. Bus rapid transit systems, which use dedicated lanes and high-capacity vehicles, can offer many of the advantages of trams at a fraction of the cost.\n\nThe debate is likely to continue. What seems clear is that the tram, once dismissed as a relic of the past, has secured a place in the future of urban transport.";
    const passage2 = "The science of smell\n\nA  Of the five senses, smell has long been regarded as the least important in humans. In the nineteenth century, the French anatomist Paul Broca classified humans as ‘non-smellers’, arguing that as our brains evolved, the areas devoted to smell had shrunk in favour of those responsible for reasoning and language. This view became widely accepted, and for much of the twentieth century smell received relatively little scientific attention.\n\nB  More recent research has challenged Broca’s conclusion. A study published in 2017 pointed out that the human olfactory bulb — the part of the brain that processes smells — is in fact relatively large, and contains a similar number of nerve cells to that of other mammals. In laboratory tests, humans have proved capable of detecting some odours at concentrations as low as those detected by dogs.\n\nC  Humans may also be able to distinguish far more smells than once believed. A frequently quoted figure, dating from the 1920s, held that people could recognise around 10,000 different odours. In 2014, researchers in New York tested volunteers with complex mixtures of chemicals and estimated that the true figure might exceed one trillion, though this estimate has since been criticised on methodological grounds.\n\nD  Smell is closely linked to memory and emotion. Unlike the other senses, signals from the nose travel directly to brain regions involved in emotion and memory, without first passing through the thalamus, which acts as a relay station for sight, hearing and touch. This may explain why a particular smell can trigger vivid memories of events that took place decades earlier, a phenomenon sometimes called the ‘Proust effect’ after the French novelist who described it.\n\nE  The importance of smell often becomes apparent only when it is lost. Anosmia, the inability to smell, affects an estimated five per cent of the population, and became much more widely discussed during the COVID-19 pandemic, when loss of smell was identified as a common symptom. People with anosmia frequently report a reduced enjoyment of food, difficulty detecting dangers such as smoke or gas, and feelings of isolation and depression.\n\nF  There is some evidence that the sense of smell can be trained. In ‘smell training’, patients sniff a set of strong odours, such as rose, lemon, eucalyptus and clove, twice a day for several months. Several studies suggest that this can help people recover their sense of smell after viral infections, although researchers caution that larger trials are needed to confirm its effectiveness.\n\nG  Industry has long recognised the power of smell. Shops, hotels and car manufacturers employ specialists to create distinctive scents intended to influence customers’ moods and behaviour. Some supermarkets release the smell of freshly baked bread near their entrances, in the belief that it encourages shoppers to buy more. Whether such techniques work as well as their advocates claim, however, is not yet clear.";
    const passage3 = "Do endangered languages matter?\n\nIt is often said that a language dies every two weeks. Whether or not this figure is precise, there is little doubt that the world’s linguistic diversity is declining rapidly. Of the roughly 7,000 languages spoken today, around 40 per cent are considered endangered, many with only a handful of elderly speakers. The question I wish to consider is not whether this decline is happening, but whether it matters — and if so, what should be done about it.\n\nSome argue that language loss is a natural process. Languages have always come and gone; Latin, after all, gave way to Italian, French and Spanish. If speakers choose to abandon their ancestral language in favour of a more widely spoken one, the argument goes, that is their right, and outsiders have no business trying to stop them. I have some sympathy with this view. It would be patronising for linguists from wealthy countries to insist that communities preserve their languages against their own wishes.\n\nHowever, the comparison with Latin is misleading. Latin did not disappear; it evolved into a family of new languages. Today’s endangered languages, by contrast, are typically being replaced entirely by a handful of global or national languages, often as a result of economic pressure, migration or, historically, deliberate government policies that punished children for speaking their mother tongue at school. In such cases, it is difficult to describe the shift as a free choice.\n\nWhat, then, is lost when a language disappears? Each language represents a distinctive way of organising experience. Some languages have no words for ‘left’ and ‘right’, describing locations instead in terms of compass directions; speakers of these languages develop a remarkable sense of orientation. Others encode detailed knowledge of local plants, animals and ecosystems, knowledge that may have practical value for medicine or conservation. When a language dies without being documented, this knowledge often dies with it.\n\nThere is also a human cost. For many communities, language is closely bound up with identity. Studies in Canada have found that indigenous communities in which a greater proportion of people speak their traditional language have lower rates of youth suicide, though researchers are careful to note that this does not prove a direct causal link.\n\nFortunately, language loss is not always irreversible. Hebrew, which had not been used as an everyday spoken language for centuries, was revived in the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries and now has millions of native speakers. Welsh, Maori and Hawaiian have all seen significant revivals as a result of immersion schools and media in those languages. These successes show that, with sufficient commitment and resources, decline can be halted.\n\nUltimately, the decision about whether to maintain a language must rest with its speakers. But governments and international organisations have a responsibility to ensure that this decision is a genuine one, by removing discrimination, funding education and supporting documentation. A language that is abandoned freely is a loss; one that is abandoned under pressure is an injustice.";
