    const passage1 = "The Great Stink\n\nIn the summer of 1858, London experienced what became known as the Great Stink. An unusually hot spell caused the River Thames, which at the time received most of the city’s untreated sewage, to give off a smell so powerful that it disrupted the work of Parliament. Curtains in the Houses of Parliament were soaked in chemicals in an attempt to mask the odour, and there was talk of moving government business out of the city altogether.\n\nThe problem had been building for decades. London’s population had grown from around one million in 1800 to more than two and a half million by the 1850s. At the same time, the introduction of the flush toilet, which had become increasingly common among wealthier households, greatly increased the volume of waste water entering the city’s drains. These drains had been designed to carry rainwater, not sewage, and they emptied directly into the Thames, the same river from which many Londoners drew their drinking water.\n\nThe consequences were deadly. London suffered repeated outbreaks of cholera in the nineteenth century, including an epidemic in 1853–54 that killed more than ten thousand people. At the time, most doctors believed that cholera was spread by ‘miasma’, or bad air. The physician John Snow had argued that the disease was in fact transmitted through contaminated water, and in 1854 he traced an outbreak in Soho to a single public water pump. His ideas, however, were not widely accepted for many years.\n\nIronically, it was the belief in miasma that finally prompted action. If foul air caused disease, then the stench rising from the Thames represented a grave danger, and Members of Parliament, who could smell it from their offices, were suddenly motivated to act. Within weeks of the Great Stink, Parliament passed legislation giving the Metropolitan Board of Works the power and funding to build a new sewer system.\n\nThe man responsible for designing it was Joseph Bazalgette, the Board’s chief engineer. His plan involved constructing a network of intercepting sewers that ran parallel to the river and carried waste eastwards, away from the centre of London, to be released into the Thames further downstream. In total, the project involved some 1,800 kilometres of street sewers feeding into 132 kilometres of main sewers.\n\nBazalgette is remembered not only for the scale of his work but for his foresight. When calculating the size of the sewers, he estimated the amount of waste likely to be produced, then doubled it, reasoning that the pipes would only be built once. This decision allowed the system to cope with London’s continued growth well into the twentieth century.\n\nThe new sewers, completed in the 1870s, transformed public health in the capital, and cholera outbreaks ceased. Today, however, the system is under strain once more. Heavy rainfall regularly causes it to overflow, releasing untreated sewage into the river. A new ‘super sewer’, the Thames Tideway Tunnel, has been built to address the problem, a twenty-first-century successor to Bazalgette’s achievement.";
    const passage2 = "The insect decline\n\nA  In 2017, a study of nature reserves in Germany made headlines around the world. Using traps that had been set up in the same locations for nearly three decades, researchers found that the total weight of flying insects caught had fallen by more than 75 per cent. The study did not identify the causes of the decline, but its findings prompted widespread alarm about what some journalists called an ‘insect apocalypse’.\n\nB  Measuring insect populations is notoriously difficult. There are estimated to be around 5.5 million insect species, of which only about one million have been formally described. Most have never been studied in any detail, and long-term data are available for only a tiny fraction of species, mostly in Europe and North America. As a result, it is hard to know whether the declines recorded in a few well-studied places are typical of the world as a whole.\n\nC  Not all studies point in the same direction. A large analysis published in 2020, which combined data from more than 1,600 sites, found that land-dwelling insects were declining at an average of around one per cent a year, while freshwater insects were actually increasing in some regions, possibly as a result of efforts to clean up rivers and lakes.\n\nD  Scientists have identified several likely causes of decline. The most important is generally considered to be habitat loss, as forests, meadows and wetlands are converted to farmland and cities. The use of pesticides, particularly a group known as neonicotinoids, has also been linked to falling numbers of bees and other insects. Climate change, light pollution and the spread of invasive species are thought to play a part as well.\n\nE  The consequences of insect decline could be serious. Insects pollinate around three-quarters of the crops grown for human food, and they are a vital source of food for birds, bats, fish and amphibians. They also play an essential role in breaking down dead plants and animals, returning nutrients to the soil. A significant loss of insects would therefore affect entire ecosystems, not just the insects themselves.\n\nF  There are, however, grounds for optimism. Insects reproduce quickly and in large numbers, which means that populations can recover rapidly if conditions improve. In the Netherlands, farmers who have left strips of wildflowers along the edges of their fields have reported significant increases in the number of pollinators within just a few years.\n\nG  Individuals can also contribute. Gardeners can plant native flowers, leave areas of grass uncut and avoid using pesticides. Some cities have begun to turn roundabouts and roadside verges into wildflower meadows, creating corridors through which insects can move between larger habitats. Such measures are unlikely to reverse global declines on their own, but they can make a measurable difference locally.";
    const passage3 = "Who owns the past?\n\nFew debates in the museum world have become as heated as the question of restitution: whether objects acquired during the colonial era should be returned to the countries or communities from which they were taken. For decades, major museums in Europe and North America resisted such calls, arguing that their collections served the whole of humanity. In recent years, however, the tide has begun to turn.\n\nThe case for restitution rests, in the first instance, on the circumstances in which many objects were acquired. A significant proportion of the African artefacts held in European museums, for example, were taken during military expeditions, often after violent conflict. The Benin Bronzes — thousands of sculptures and plaques removed from the Kingdom of Benin, in present-day Nigeria, by British troops in 1897 — are perhaps the best-known example. It is difficult to argue that objects seized in such circumstances were acquired legitimately, whatever the legal position at the time.\n\nMuseums have traditionally offered several arguments against returning such objects. One is that they are better able to look after them, with specialist conservation staff and climate-controlled storage. Another is that collections bringing together objects from many cultures allow visitors to make comparisons and to appreciate the diversity of human achievement. A third, more practical argument is that if every object with a disputed history were returned, the great encyclopaedic museums would be emptied.\n\nI find the first of these arguments the least persuasive. It assumes that institutions in the countries of origin are incapable of caring for their own heritage, an assumption that is both outdated and, frankly, condescending. Nigeria, for example, has developed plans for a major new museum to house returned Benin Bronzes. Where genuine concerns exist about storage or security, these can be addressed through partnership rather than used as a reason for refusal.\n\nThe second argument has more merit. There is real value in being able to see objects from different cultures side by side, and in making them accessible to large numbers of visitors. But this value does not depend on ownership. Long-term loans, travelling exhibitions and digital reproductions can all allow objects to be seen widely while acknowledging that they belong elsewhere.\n\nAs for the fear of empty museums, it seems to me exaggerated. Most claims for restitution concern specific objects with well-documented histories of violent or coercive acquisition, not entire collections. Moreover, museums that have returned objects have often found that the process strengthens their relationships with communities of origin, leading to new collaborations, research and exhibitions.\n\nSeveral European governments have now committed to returning objects taken during the colonial period. Germany began returning Benin Bronzes to Nigeria in 2022, and other institutions have followed. These moves have not been without complications, including disagreements within countries of origin about who should receive returned objects. Nevertheless, they represent a significant shift in thinking.\n\nRestitution is not simply about the transfer of objects from one building to another. It is about acknowledging the injustices of the past and recognising that the right to tell the story of an object belongs, at least in part, to the people who made it.";
