    const passage1 = "Guiding lights\n\nFor centuries, lighthouses have guided ships safely along dangerous coastlines. The most famous early example was the Pharos of Alexandria, built in Egypt in the third century BC. Standing perhaps more than 100 metres tall, it was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, and is thought to have used a fire at its summit, possibly reflected by mirrors, to produce light visible far out at sea. It was badly damaged by a series of earthquakes in the Middle Ages and had disappeared completely by the fifteenth century.\n\nFor much of history, lighthouses relied on burning wood, coal or candles, which produced a weak and unreliable light. Keeping these fires alight, particularly in storms, was difficult and dangerous work. In the late eighteenth century, the introduction of oil lamps with reflectors brought significant improvements, but the light produced was still limited.\n\nThe most important breakthrough came in 1822, when the French physicist Augustin-Jean Fresnel invented a new type of lens. Instead of a single thick piece of glass, which would have been extremely heavy, Fresnel’s lens consisted of a series of concentric rings of glass, each designed to bend light in the same direction. The result was a lens that could concentrate the light from a lamp into a powerful beam, visible from more than 30 kilometres away. Fresnel lenses were soon adopted around the world, and many remain in use today.\n\nLighthouses also needed to be distinguishable from one another, so that sailors could identify their position at night. This was achieved by giving each lighthouse a distinctive pattern of flashes, known as its ‘characteristic’. Some lighthouses flash once every few seconds; others produce groups of flashes, or alternate between different colours. During the day, lighthouses can be identified by their shape and by the patterns painted on their towers, such as stripes or bands.\n\nThe job of lighthouse keeper was often lonely and demanding. Keepers had to maintain the light throughout the night, clean the lenses, and record weather conditions. On remote rock lighthouses, they might spend weeks at a time without seeing anyone other than their fellow keepers. Many were accompanied by their families, and some lighthouse keepers’ wives took over the job after their husbands’ deaths.\n\nIn the twentieth century, technology gradually made the keeper’s role unnecessary. Electric lamps replaced oil, and automatic systems allowed lights to be switched on and off without human intervention. In the United Kingdom, the last manned lighthouse was automated in 1998. Today, satellite navigation systems have reduced ships’ dependence on lighthouses, although they remain an important back-up in case electronic systems fail.\n\nMany former lighthouses have found new uses as museums, holiday accommodation and even wedding venues, preserving buildings that remain powerful symbols of safety and guidance.";
    const passage2 = "Meat without animals?\n\nA  Walk into almost any large supermarket today and you will find shelves of burgers, sausages and chicken pieces made entirely from plants. Plant-based meat alternatives are not new — tofu has been eaten in East Asia for more than a thousand years, and vegetarian sausages have been available in Europe for decades — but the products now on sale are designed to imitate the taste and texture of animal meat far more closely than their predecessors.\n\nB  Much of this progress has come from food science. Manufacturers use proteins extracted from soya, peas and wheat, which are heated and pressed through machines in a process called extrusion to create fibres resembling muscle tissue. Some companies add a molecule called heme, produced by genetically modified yeast, to give their products a ‘meaty’ flavour and a pink colour that changes as the burger cooks.\n\nC  The environmental case for plant-based alternatives is strong. Livestock farming is responsible for a significant share of global greenhouse gas emissions, as well as large amounts of land and water use. Studies suggest that producing a plant-based burger generates a fraction of the emissions associated with a beef burger, although the exact figures vary depending on the methods used.\n\nD  Health claims are more contested. Plant-based meats contain no cholesterol and are often high in protein and fibre. However, many are highly processed and contain significant amounts of salt, fat and additives. Nutritionists point out that a plant-based burger is not necessarily healthier than a lean piece of meat, and that whole foods such as beans and lentils remain a better choice.\n\nE  After rapid growth in the late 2010s, sales in some markets have levelled off or even declined. Analysts suggest several reasons: prices remain higher than for conventional meat, some consumers are put off by long ingredient lists, and the novelty that attracted early buyers has faded. Companies that once promised to replace animal meat entirely have scaled back their ambitions.\n\nF  Meanwhile, a different technology is attracting attention: cultivated meat, grown from animal cells in large tanks called bioreactors. In 2020, Singapore became the first country to approve the sale of cultivated chicken. Producing such meat at scale and at an affordable price remains a major challenge, however, and some governments have moved to restrict or ban its sale.\n\nG  It seems unlikely that any single solution will transform the way the world eats. Instead, experts expect a gradual shift, with plant-based and cultivated products taking a growing share of the market alongside traditional meat. Much will depend on price: when alternatives become cheaper than the products they imitate, consumer behaviour is likely to change far more quickly.";
    const passage3 = "In defence of learning by heart\n\nLearning poems or passages by heart was once a central part of education. Generations of schoolchildren recited verses, multiplication tables and lists of kings and queens until they were word perfect. In recent decades, however, memorisation has fallen out of favour. It is often dismissed as ‘rote learning’ — mechanical repetition without understanding — and contrasted unfavourably with approaches that emphasise critical thinking and creativity. With search engines able to supply any fact in seconds, the argument goes, there is little point in storing information in our heads.\n\nI believe this argument is mistaken, or at least seriously incomplete. It rests on a false opposition between memory and understanding. In reality, the two are deeply connected. Cognitive scientists have shown that our ability to think about a subject depends heavily on what we already know about it. Knowledge stored in long-term memory frees up our limited working memory to deal with new information and to make connections. A student who has to look up every word or fact is likely to find complex reasoning far more difficult than one who has a rich store of knowledge to draw upon.\n\nThis is not to defend mindless repetition. Memorising information that one does not understand is of limited value, and can make learning unpleasant. But memorisation and understanding can and should support each other. A student who has learned a poem by heart is able to return to it again and again, noticing patterns of sound and meaning that a single reading would miss. Many people report that poems they learned as children have stayed with them throughout their lives, offering comfort and insight at difficult moments.\n\nThere are also practical benefits. Memorisation requires concentration and perseverance, qualities that are valuable in many areas of life. Some research suggests that practising retrieval — trying to recall information rather than simply rereading it — is one of the most effective ways to learn, a finding sometimes called the ‘testing effect’. Learning by heart is, in essence, an intensive form of retrieval practice.\n\nCritics are right to point out that memorisation can be stressful for some students, particularly when it is linked to high-stakes examinations. Teachers should be sensitive to this, and should present learning by heart as an achievement rather than a test. They should also choose material carefully: a short poem that students enjoy is likely to be more beneficial than a long list of dates.\n\nI do not claim that memorisation should return to the dominant position it once held. A curriculum based entirely on recitation would be narrow and dull. But to abandon it altogether would be to discard a powerful tool. The ability to carry knowledge in our heads, rather than in our pockets, is part of what it means to be educated.\n\nIn an age of instant information, it may seem old-fashioned to ask students to learn things by heart. But what we know shapes how we think. That, surely, is reason enough.";
